{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "McKenney1970-Fig2-passband-500-620",
  "Source": "D. B. McKenney and P. N. Slater, \"Design and Use of Interference Passband Filters with Wide-Angle Lenses for Multispectral Photography,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2435-2440 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002435 - Fig. 2 caption, p. 2436: A lambda1(H/2 L H/2)^7 M lambda2(H/2 L H/2)^7 H' G; text p. 2436 ('thirty-two-layer filter')",
  "Notes": "32-layer visible passband filter (about 500-620 nm) for multispectral photography, compared with a Wratten 57 absorption filter\nSpecification: Rectangular passband ~500-620 nm with high transmittance; rejection by the first-order high-reflection bands of two [H/2 L H/2]^7 stacks centred at 411.3 nm and 730.0 nm.\nReported: Fig. 2 (normal incidence, computed): passband transmittance ~0.95-0.97 from ~510 to ~575 nm, a dip to ~0.88-0.90 near ~585-590 nm, back to ~0.95 near ~605 nm, steep edges with half-power points near ~495 nm and ~615-620 nm, T < 0.05 below ~470 nm and above ~640 nm (read off plot, approximate). Fig. 7(a) dashed curve (flat filter, normal incidence, unpolarized): half-power points about 0.485-0.49 um and 0.615 um. Fig. 4 (p. 2437): long-wavelength cutoff shifts by 12 nm (2 %) at 20 deg angle of incidence, roughly proportional to 1-(cos theta)^1/2. Fig. 5: at 45 deg the p-polarization passband extends to ~595-600 nm (half power) with T ~0.95, while s-polarization cuts off near ~555-560 nm with a peak ~0.9 near 510-520 nm (read off plot). Fig. 8: long-wave half-power point of the filter on lens surfaces at 0 deg semifield is about 610-617 nm.\nNotes: Paper lists the stack from the incident medium A (n = 1.0) to the substrate G (n = 1.52), same order as here. Each (H/2 L H/2)^7 is expanded and adjacent H/2 halves merged: H/2, (L H)x6, L, H/2 = 15 layers; 15 + M + 15 + H' = 32 layers, matching the paper's 'thirty-two-layer filter'. Stack 1 is quarterwave at lambda1 = 411.3 nm, stack 2 at lambda2 = 730.0 nm; M and H' have (4nh) = 553.6 nm. Physical thicknesses computed as lambda/(4n) (H/2 as lambda/(8n)), rounded to 0.1 pm: stack 1 H 44.7065, H/2 22.3533, L 74.5109 nm; stack 2 H 79.3478, H/2 39.6739, L 132.2464 nm; M 65.9048 nm; H' 60.1739 nm. Assumption: H' uses nH = 2.30 (the caption gives no separate nH'; Fig. 1 suggests an ideal external matching index near 2.4, but only nH = 2.30 is printed). The last H/2 of stack 2 and H' are then the same material and physically merge into one 99.8478 nm layer; they are kept separate to match the paper's layer count. Non-dispersive, lossless indices; the paper names MgF2, ZrO2 and ZnS for indices ~1.4, 2.1, 2.4 but the caption gives only numbers, so constant-index materials are used. reference_nm 553.6 is the matching-layer wavelength. Merit operands are illustrative (the design is analytical, not optimised). Unpolarized (mean of s and p) at oblique angles.\nLayers from Fig. 2 caption, p. 2436: A lambda1(H/2 L H/2)^7 M lambda2(H/2 L H/2)^7 H' G; text p. 2436 ('thirty-two-layer filter'); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours half-power points 500 and 618 nm; paper about 495 and 615-620 nm (Fig. 2).",
